text:
maria_dev: Trying to get the new JS sample app running on Cloudflare Workers and I'm hitting runtime errors: `ReferenceError: require is not defined` and later `Error: Cannot find module 'fs'`. The sample in /examples/worker uses CJS + node-fetch. Anyone else run into this on a fresh wrangler deploy?

samir_dx: Yep. CF Workers are ESM-only and don't have Node built-ins. Our example repo still has a legacy CJS build that pulls in node-fetch and buffer. You need an edge/browser build that uses globalThis.fetch and Web Crypto.

ida_engineer: Short workaround: switch package.json to `"type": "module"`, remove any `require()` and use `import` for the SDK. Also replace any use of `Buffer`/`process.env` with browser-friendly alternatives.

maria_dev: That helped but auth code still fails. Our middleware does `const token = process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY` and uses `node-abort-controller` for retries. Workers don't expose process.env and have AbortController globally. What's the recommended pattern for secret management + retries in edge environments? :thinking_face:

samir_dx: For secrets use Wrangler/Terraform secrets (they end up on globalThis.__STATIC_CONTENT or env vars exposed via wrangler). For SDKs we should detect the runtime and read from `globalThis` or accept an injected `fetch` + `getSecret` function. Retrying should rely on the global AbortController + the platform's fetch.

ida_engineer: Minimal edge auth adapter we added in my branch — something like:```js
// edge-adapter.js
export function createEdgeClient({ apiKey, fetchImpl = globalThis.fetch }) {
  return async function call(payload) {
    const res = await fetchImpl('https://api.redwood-inference.example/v1/generate', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json', 'Authorization': `Bearer ${apiKey}` },
      body: JSON.stringify(payload),
    });
    return res.json();
  };
}
```
This avoids node-fetch and doesn't touch process.

alex_docs: Heads-up: our published type definitions leak `NodeJS.ReadableStream` and `Buffer` in the top-level index.d.ts. That causes `tsc` errors in worker projects. We should ship an `edge.d.ts` that only references DOM types (RequestInit, Headers, ReadableStream from lib.dom).

maria_dev: Agree — the DX hit is rough. New users hit runtime errors, then typescript fails, then we get support tickets. For onboarding, can we add a CI check that attempts to bundle the example with `wrangler` and run `tsc --lib dom`?

samir_dx: +1. Also we should publish a dual-build: `dist/node.cjs.js` (for Node) and `dist/edge.esm.js` (for Workers/browser). package.json map fields: `exports\": { ".": { \"import\": \"./dist/edge.esm.js\", \"require\": \"./dist/node.cjs.js\" } }`. For types: `types` -> `dist/types/index.d.ts` and add a `types.edge` entry (or `types` could point to union—better to generate two `*.d.ts` and document which to import).

ida_engineer: Small gotcha: bundlers might still polyfill `global` -> `globalThis` mismatch. In the worker example we added at top: `globalThis.global = globalThis;` to cover old libs, but that's hacky. We should instead remove reliance on `global` in the SDK code.

deploy-bot: build:example-worker failed on staging (bundle size 1.15MB, limit 1MB).

maria_dev: Oof. Tree-shake + remove node polyfills should get us under the limit. Also we can move heavy stuff to dedicated hosted builds.

alex_docs: Action items: 1) PR from @ida_engineer to merge edge adapter + example change, 2) update package.json exports for dual-build, 3) generate `edge.d.ts` and surface in docs, 4) add wrangler+tsc step to example CI. I can update docs and example README once PRs land.

samir_dx: I'll pick the dual-build and export map. Will add runtime detection helper: `isEdge = typeof Deno !== 'undefined' || (typeof globalThis.fetch === 'function' && !globalThis.process)`.

maria_dev: Thanks folks. If we get those in this week I can re-run the onboarding test with customer-reported sample. :thumbsup:
